[{'code': "fig = px.bar(funding_by_institution, x='institution', y='funding', "
          "color='institution', title='Institutional Funding Distribution')",
  'explanation': 'The chart is a bar plot where the x-axis represents '
                 "'institution' and the y-axis represents 'funding'. Each "
                 'institution is represented by a different color. The goal of '
                 'this chart is to visualize the distribution of funding '
                 'across various institutions, with the height of each bar '
                 'indicating the total funding received by that institution. '
                 'The main insights from the chart include identifying which '
                 'institutions receive the most funding.',
  'section': 'accessibility'},
 {'code': "data['funding'] = pd.to_numeric(data['funding'], errors='coerce')\n"
          '\n'
          "data = data[pd.notna(data['funding'])]\n"
          '\n'
          'funding_by_institution = '
          "data.groupby('institution')['funding'].sum().reset_index()\n"
          '\n'
          'funding_by_institution = '
          "funding_by_institution.sort_values(by='funding', ascending=False)",
  'explanation': 'This section of the code performs several transformations on '
                 "the input DataFrame. First, it converts the 'funding' column "
                 'to numeric values, coercing any non-numeric entries to NaN. '
                 "Then, it removes rows with NaN values in the 'funding' "
                 "column. Next, it groups the data by 'institution' and sums "
                 'the funding amounts for each institution. Finally, it sorts '
                 'the resulting DataFrame by the total funding in descending '
                 'order.',
  'section': 'transformation'},
 {'code': "fig = px.bar(funding_by_institution, x='institution', y='funding', "
          "color='institution', title='Institutional Funding Distribution')",
  'explanation': 'This line of code creates a bar chart using Plotly Express. '
                 "The 'x' parameter is set to 'institution', which means the "
                 "institutions are plotted on the x-axis. The 'y' parameter is "
                 "set to 'funding', indicating that funding amounts will be "
